Subject: compiler-cranelift, compiler: keep the unwind information of
 trampolines

Cranelift describes the frames of the function call trampolines, through
which the host calls into a module, and of the dynamic trampolines,
through which a module calls a host function, as it does the module's
own functions. Both generators passed that description through
maybe_into_to_windows_unwind, which keeps only the Windows form, so on a
System V target a trampoline had no unwind information: the module's
.eh_frame covered its functions but not the frames between them and the
host, and a DWARF unwinder, the runtime's own backtraces or a profiler's,
stopped at the first trampoline it reached.

The generators now return a trampoline with its System V FDE, and the
module places it with the rest. A traditional artifact's frame table
gains an FDE for every trampoline, under a CIE of their own with no
personality or LSDA, since no exception is handled in a trampoline. The
table is built for any module with imports or signatures, not only for
one with function bodies, because such a module still has trampolines.
A dynamic trampoline's FDE is relocated against the existing
DynamicTrampoline target; a call trampoline had none, so relocations
gain a FunctionCallTrampoline target, which linking resolves to the
allocated trampoline and object emission to its symbol. In a native ELF
artifact each trampoline object carries its own .eh_frame section, as
each function's does.

The new relocation target changes the archived format, so the metadata
version changes with it, but not to 24, which a later upstream format
would likely claim. The version's low byte is the upstream version the
format extends, 23, and its upper bytes spell "SC", far beyond where
upstream's count of versions is likely to reach. An artifact serialized
under any other version is refused as incompatible and compiled again.

Unit tests cover a module with one import and no function bodies getting
FDEs for both of its trampolines, relocated against each, and x86-64 and
AArch64 trampoline objects each carrying one relocatable FDE.

